You can handle multiple topics using a single Kafka consumer in dotnet C#. When you subscribe the consumer to the topics, you can specify all the topics you want to listen to in a list. The consumer will then listen for messages from all the specified topics. Here's an example:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Threading;
using Confluent.Kafka;

namespace KafkaConsumerSample
{
    public class Program
    {
        public static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            var conf = new ConsumerConfig
            {
                GroupId = "test-consumer-group",
                BootstrapServers = "localhost:9092",
                AutoOffsetReset = AutoOffsetReset.Earliest
            };

            using (var consumer = new ConsumerBuilder<Ignore, string>(conf).Build())
            {
                var topics = new List<string> { "topic1", "topic2", "topic3" };
                consumer.Subscribe(topics);

                CancellationTokenSource cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
                Console.CancelKeyPress += (_, e) => {
                    e.Cancel = true; // Prevent the process from terminating.
                    cts.Cancel();
                };

                try
                {
                    while (true)
                    {
                        try
                        {
                            var consumeResult = consumer.Consume(cts.Token);
                            Console.WriteLine($"Message '{consumeResult.Message.Value}' consumed from topic '{consumeResult.Topic}', partition {consumeResult.Partition}, offset {consumeResult.Offset}");
                        }
                        catch (ConsumeException e)
                        {
                            Console.WriteLine($"Error consuming message: {e.Error.Reason}");
                        }
                    }
                }
                catch (OperationCanceledException)
                {
                    // Ensure the consumer leaves the consumer group cleanly and final offsets are committed.
                    consumer.Close();
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

This example shows how to create a single consumer that subscribes and processes messages from three different topics, "topic1", "topic2", and "topic3". If the message processing takes a long time or you need to process messages in parallel, you can implement multi-threading based on your requirements.

If you require separate processing logics for each topic, you might want to write separate consumers for better code organization and separation of concerns. It really depends on your specific use case and processing requirements.

