import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.Set;

class Solution {
    public int solution(int[] A) {
        Set<Integer> set = new HashSet<>();

        for (int num : A) {
            if (num > 0) {
                set.add(num);
            }
        }

        for (int i = 1; i <= 100001; i++) {
            if (!set.contains(i)) {
                return i;
            }
        }

        return -1;
    }
}
```

This function imports a Set (HashSet) to store the positive integers found in the input array A. It iterates through the array of integers (num) and adds them into the Set if they are greater than 0 (positive integers).

Next, the function iterates over the range from 1 to 100,001 (covering the maximum possible range of unique positive integers considering the given constraints), checking if the current number (i) is in the Set. If it's not, the function returns the current number.

Finally, the function returns -1 if it didn't find a missing positive integer. This shouldn't happen under the given constraints but is included as an edge case.

