In a Spring Boot, Spring Data JPA, and MongoDB environment, you can perform efficient paging queries without using an offset. Instead of the offset-based approach, you can use the "seek method" for pagination. This method relies on the last value of the previous page as the starting point for the next page.

Here's an example of how to implement this using Spring Boot and Spring Data JPA:

1. Firstly, create an Entity class:

```java
import org.springframework.data.annotation.Id;
import org.springframework.data.mongodb.core.mapping.Document;

@Document(collection = "items")
public class Item {

    @Id
    private String id;
    private String name;
    // other fields

    // Getters and Setters
}
```

2. Create a Repository interface that extends from `MongoRepository`:

```java
import org.springframework.data.domain.Page;
import org.springframework.data.domain.Pageable;
import org.springframework.data.mongodb.repository.MongoRepository;
import org.springframework.data.mongodb.repository.Query;

public interface ItemRepository extends MongoRepository<Item, String> {

    @Query("{ '_id': { $gt: ?0 } }")
    Page<Item> findByItemsAfterId(String lastItemId, Pageable pageable);
}
```

3. Now, in your Service class, add a method that gets the page of items based on the last item ID:

```java
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.data.domain.Page;
import org.springframework.data.domain.PageRequest;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class ItemService {

    @Autowired
    private ItemRepository itemRepository;

    public Page<Item> getItemsPage(String lastItemId, int pageSize) {
        return itemRepository.findByItemsAfterId(lastItemId, PageRequest.of(0, pageSize));
    }
}
```

4. In your Controller class, add an endpoint that receives the `lastItemId` and `pageSize` as query parameters:

```java
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.data.domain.Page;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RequestParam;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;

@RestController
public class ItemController {

    @Autowired
    private ItemService itemService;

    @GetMapping("/items")
    public Page<Item> getItems(@RequestParam(value = "lastItemId", required = false) String lastItemId,
                               @RequestParam(value = "pageSize", defaultValue = "10") int pageSize) {
        return itemService.getItemsPage(lastItemId, pageSize);
    }
}
```

Now, when you call the `/items` endpoint, it will return a page of items without using an offset. Instead, it will use the provided `lastItemId` as the starting point for the next page, making it a more efficient pagination solution.

Yes, you can test validation just before saving it in C#. With clean architecture best practices, you should separate your validation logic from the data access layer. One way to achieve this is by implementing the repository pattern and the use of Domain Entities.

In this setup, you would have a separate method for validation, which you could call before saving the object to the database. Here's a basic example:

1. Create a Domain Entity:

```csharp
public class User
{
    public int Id { get; set; }
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public string Email { get; set; }

    public bool IsValid()
