chosen:
To sort the list of movies based on their ratings in descending order, you can use the `sort` function in MATLAB. Since your movies and ratings are stored in a single matrix, you may want to separate them into two arrays (cell and numeric) and then sort the ratings while keeping track of the original indices. Then, you can use the sorted indices to sort the movie names.

Here's the code to sort the movies based on their ratings in descending order:

```MATLAB
% Separate movies and ratings
movie_names = ["The Shawshank Redemption", "The Godfather", "The Dark Knight", "12 Angry Men", "Schindler's List"];
ratings = [9.3, 9.2, 9.0, 8.9, 8.9];

% Sort the ratings in descending order and retain their indices
[sorted_ratings, idx] = sort(ratings, 'descend');

% Sort the movie names based on the sorted indices
sorted_movie_names = movie_names(idx);
```

To suggest the top 3 movies based on their ratings for your movie night, you can simply select the first 3 movies and ratings from the sorted arrays:

```MATLAB
% Suggest top 3 movies
top_movies = sorted_movie_names(1:3);
top_ratings = sorted_ratings(1:3);

% Display the top 3 movies and their ratings
disp('Top 3 Movies for Movie Night:');
for i = 1:length(top_movies)
    fprintf('%s (Rating: %.1f)\n', top_movies(i), top_ratings(i));
end
```

