As of now, Node.js 18 has not been released, so I can't provide you with a Node.js 18 specific example. However, I can show you how to make an HTTP POST request using `fetch` in a current Node.js environment. Please note that `fetch` is not available in Node.js by default, so you'll need to use a package like `node-fetch` to use the `fetch` function.

First, you'll need to install the `node-fetch` package via npm:

```
npm install node-fetch
```

Then, here's an example on how to use `fetch` for an HTTP POST request in Node.js:

```javascript
const fetch = require('node-fetch');

async function httpPostExample() {
  const url = 'https://api.example.com/data';
  const data = {
    key1: 'value1',
    key2: 'value2'
  };

  const response = await fetch(url, {
    method: 'POST', // Specify the request method
    headers: {
      'Content-Type': 'application/json' // Set the content type of the request body
    },
    body: JSON.stringify(data) // Convert the data object to a JSON string
  });

  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error(`HTTP error! Status: ${response.status}`);
  }

  const jsonResponse = await response.json(); // Parse the response as JSON
  console.log(jsonResponse); // Display the JSON response
}

httpPostExample()
  .catch(error => console.error('Error occurred while making the POST request:', error));
```

Replace 'https://api.example.com/data' with the actual API endpoint you want to send a POST request to and modify the `data` object according to your needs.

