To check session variables in an ASP.NET 4.0 project and add dynamic styles to a div tag, you can follow the steps below:

1. In your ASP.NET 4.0 code, first check if the session variable exists. You can do this by using the `Session` keyword in ASP.NET:
```csharp
if (Session["myVariableName"] != null)
{
    // The session variable is set, proceed with the dynamic styling
}
```
In this example, replace "myVariableName" with the name of your session variable.

1. Once you've confirmed that the session variable is set, you can then apply the dynamic styles to the div tag:
```csharp
if (Session["myVariableName"] != null)
{
    Response.Write("<style type='text/css'>");
    Response.Write("div#myDivID { color: " + Session["myVariableName"] + "; }");
    Response.Write("</style>");
}
```
In this example, replace "myDivID" with the ID of your div tag, and "myVariableName" with the name of your session variable that contains the color value you want to apply to the div's text.

1. Alternatively, you can also use inline CSS styles:
```csharp
if (Session["myVariableName"] != null)
{
    Response.Write("<div id='myDivID' style='color:" + Session["myVariableName"] + "'>");
    // Your content goes here
    Response.Write("</div>");
}
```
Remember that by using this method, you're writing the CSS inline directly into the HTML page. It's generally recommended to use an external CSS file and avoid inline styles, but in this case, it's warranted due to the dynamic nature of the styles being generated based on the session variable.

